<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

CAT-SOOP courses are directory trees: the course directory is the home page,
and subdirectories become child pages.  Configuration in `preload.py` follows
the same tree and is inherited by the pages below it.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Course and Page Directories</catsoop-section>

CAT-SOOP finds courses under the `courses` subdirectory of its configured data
directory (`cs_data_root`).  A course can be stored there directly:

```text
/path/to/catsoop-data/
└── courses/
    └── numerical_methods/
        ├── preload.py
        ├── content.catsoop
        ├── __STATIC__/
        ├── __USERS__/
        ├── lectures/
        │   ├── content.catsoop
        │   └── lecture01/
        │       ├── preload.py
        │       └── content.catsoop
        └── homework/
            └── hw01/
                ├── preload.py
                └── content.catsoop
```

It can also be kept in a separate repository and linked into that directory:

```sh
ln -s /path/to/numerical-methods-course \
    /path/to/catsoop-data/courses/numerical_methods
```

The directory name, `numerical_methods` above, is the course's short name and
the first component of its URL.  For example, the `hw01` page would be
available at:

```text
https://catsoop.example/numerical_methods/homework/hw01
```

Each page directory normally contains `content.catsoop`.  That file holds the
page's prose, Python blocks, and questions.  Subdirectories create child pages.
Files that should be served without being interpreted as page source, such as
images or downloads, belong in an appropriate `__STATIC__` directory.
Course-specific user records can be placed in `__USERS__`; custom qtypes and
plugins use other specially named directories such as `__QTYPES__` and
`__PLUGINS__`.

<catsoop-section>Choosing a Page Source File</catsoop-section>

CAT-SOOP recognizes four page-source extensions:

* **`.catsoop`** is Markdown plus CAT-SOOP tags such as `<question>` and
  `<python>`.  `content.catsoop` is the clearest default for an ordinary course
  page because its name tells editors and readers that the file contains more
  than plain Markdown.
* **`.md`** is processed in the same way as `.catsoop`.  Use `content.md` when
  Markdown-oriented editing, preview, or repository tooling is more useful
  than the CAT-SOOP-specific extension.
* **`.xml`** skips Markdown conversion and treats the source as HTML/XML-like
  CAT-SOOP markup.  Use `content.xml` for a page intentionally written as raw
  HTML with CAT-SOOP tags.
* **`.py`** is executed as a Python page program.  It builds the page by
  printing HTML or by setting CAT-SOOP context variables.  Use it for a highly
  dynamic page or a small request-driven endpoint, not for prose that would be
  clearer as Markdown.

A directory page uses one of these conventional filenames:

```text
topic/
├── preload.py
└── content.catsoop
```

`content.md`, `content.xml`, or `content.py` can replace `content.catsoop`.
Keep only one `content.*` source in a directory so it is unambiguous which
format defines the page.

CAT-SOOP also recognizes a source file itself as a page.  For example:

```text
numerical_methods/
├── preload.py
├── content.catsoop
├── page.md
└── program.py
```

This creates `/numerical_methods/page` from `page.md` and
`/numerical_methods/program` from `program.py`; the extension is omitted from
the URL.  A single-file page can likewise use `.catsoop` or `.xml`.

Use a single file such as `page.md` for a small leaf page that needs no
local configuration or assets.  Use a directory with `content.*` when the page
needs its own `preload.py`, `__STATIC__` files, or child pages.  A single-file
page inherits the `preload.py` files from its containing directory, but it
cannot have a preload belonging only to that file.

A Python page is ordinary server-side Python.  CAT-SOOP captures `print`
output as page content:

```python
# program.py
print("<h2>Course status</h2>")

if cs_form.get("detail") == "full":
    print("<p>Displaying the full status report.</p>")
else:
    print("<p>Displaying the summary.</p>")
```

Python-page output is HTML, not Markdown.  Since the whole file is executable,
reserve `.py` pages for cases where request handling or programmatic generation
is the main purpose of the page.

<catsoop-section>What preload.py Does</catsoop-section>

`preload.py` contains ordinary Python that configures a course or page.
CAT-SOOP runs the course's top-level `preload.py` first, followed by each
`preload.py` on the path to the requested page.  Values therefore inherit down
the directory tree, and a more specific page can override a parent setting.

The root file is a good place for course-wide settings:

```python
cs_long_name = "Numerical Methods"
cs_title = "Numerical Methods"
cs_header = "Numerical Methods"
cs_default_role = "Student"
cs_show_section_permalinks = True
```

A page-level file should contain only settings local to that page:

```python
# homework/hw01/preload.py
cs_long_name = "Homework 1: Simulation"
cs_release_date = "2026-07-14:09:00"
cs_due_date = "2026-07-20:23:00"
```

Preloads can also define shared helper functions, checker functions, navigation,
sandbox settings, and other course configuration.  They run before the page's
`content.catsoop` is evaluated.  Because preload processing happens before
course-specific user information is loaded, avoid making preload behavior
depend directly on the current user's `cs_user_info`.

<catsoop-section>Roles and Permissions</catsoop-section>

Role names are course configuration, not a fixed hierarchy built into
CAT-SOOP.  A course maps each role to a list of permissions with
`cs_permissions`.  Common conventions are:

* **Admin** and **Instructor**: broad course administration and grading;
* **TA**, **UTA**, or **LA**: staff roles with the permissions appropriate to
  their responsibilities;
* **Student**: view released pages and submit work;
* **Guest**: view released pages without submitting;
* **Unauthenticated**: assigned automatically when nobody is logged in.

A small course might start with:

```python
cs_default_role = "Guest"

cs_permissions = {
    "Admin": [
        "view_all", "submit_all", "impersonate", "admin", "grade"
    ],
    "Instructor": [
        "view_all", "submit_all", "impersonate", "admin", "grade"
    ],
    "TA": ["view_all", "submit_all", "impersonate", "grade"],
    "Student": ["view", "submit"],
    "Guest": ["view"],
    "Unauthenticated": [],
}
```

The most common permissions are:

* `view` and `submit`, which respect release and due-date rules;
* `view_all` and `submit_all`, which let staff bypass those timing rules;
* `grade`, which allows grading;
* `impersonate`, which allows viewing the course as another user;
* `admin`, which enables administrative operations.

Give each role only the permissions it needs.  Authentication may supply a
role, or a course can assign one in `__USERS__/USERNAME.py`.  See
[Defining Course Users](COURSE/docs/authoring/users) for user-file details.

If an authenticated user has no explicit role, CAT-SOOP uses
`cs_default_role`.

<catsoop-section>Course Start, Release, and Due Dates</catsoop-section>

Absolute dates use the form `YYYY-MM-DD:HH:MM`, in the CAT-SOOP server's local
time:

```python
cs_release_date = "2026-07-16:09:00"
cs_due_date = "2026-07-20:23:00"
```

`cs_release_date` controls when users with ordinary `view` permission can see a
page.  Its default is `"ALWAYS"`.  Staff with `view_all` can see an unreleased
page.

`cs_due_date` controls when work is considered due.  Its default is `"NEVER"`.
CAT-SOOP normally continues to accept late submissions; set
`cs_auto_lock = True` when submission should stop at the due date.  Staff with
`submit_all` are not bound by the ordinary student limit.

CAT-SOOP does not have a separate general-purpose `cs_start_date`.  For courses
that use week-relative schedules, `cs_first_monday` serves as the course-start
anchor:

```python
# Course preload.py
cs_first_monday = "2026-07-13:00:00"

# A page preload.py
cs_week_number = 1
cs_release_date = "R:09:00"   # Thursday of week 1
cs_due_date = "M+:23:00"      # Monday immediately after week 1
```

The weekday letters are `M`, `T`, `W`, `R`, `F`, `S`, and `U`; trailing `+`
and `-` characters move forward or backward by weeks.  Many courses use
absolute dates for individual pages because they are easiest to audit.
Whichever style is used, keep dates in the page's `preload.py` so timing
metadata stays next to the page it controls.
